damaged_code:
```rust
// One DB model holding two distinct domain concepts plus a subtype hierarchy,
// with nothing preventing a row whose fields contradict its discriminator.
#[derive(DBTable)]
struct Party {
    id: u64,
    party_type: String,                  // "Person" | "Company" | "Contractor"
    first_name: Option<String>,          // Person only
    registration_number: Option<String>, // Company only
    hourly_rate: Option<Decimal>,        // Contractor only
    monthly_salary: Option<Decimal>,     // Person only
}
// No CHECK constraint: a "Company" row may carry first_name and hourly_rate,
// and a "Contractor" row may have hourly_rate = NULL.
```

fix:
For distinct concepts, use separate concrete tables when that reflects their ownership and lifecycle. For a real hierarchy, choose Class Table Inheritance (one table per type sharing a primary key) or Concrete Table Inheritance (separate tables per type) as appropriate, preserving required subtype and cross-table invariants. When a low-complexity single table remains justified, enforce discriminator-dependent required/forbidden fields with database constraints (e.g., conditional CHECK constraints) instead of splitting solely because columns are nullable.
```rust
struct Person {
    id: u64,
    first_name: String,
}

struct Company {
    id: u64,
    registration_number: String,
}
```
